        <!-- ===================== §5 · WHAT IT COVERS ===================== -->
        <section class="sec" id="covers">
          <h2>What Does a Real African Grey Health Guarantee Actually Cover?</h2>
          <p class="opener">A written health guarantee is a contract about risk, and here at C.A.Gs ours names the risks it accepts instead of gesturing at them. It stands behind two categories of illness for 72 hours after your grey lands, so that a fault carried out of our aviary stays our responsibility rather than becoming your veterinary bill.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "what-african-grey-health-guarantee-covers.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "what-african-grey-health-guarantee-covers-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "what-african-grey-health-guarantee-covers.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="What our written African Grey health guarantee covers, and the four conditions that void it">

          <h3>The Conditions Our Written Guarantee Stands Behind</h3>
          <p>Two categories carry the weight. A congenital defect is something a grey hatched with — a heart or airway fault, a skeletal problem — that a pre-sale examination had no way of surfacing. Infectious disease covers an organism already at work in your bird when the crate closed but not yet visible to a vet. Both share one quality: they existed before you did anything, which is precisely why they belong to us.</p>

          <h4>Which Illnesses Fall Inside the Guarantee Window?</h4>
          <p>Anything a veterinarian can trace to a point before delivery falls inside. In practice that means a positive PCR result on a disease we screen for, or a structural fault identified on examination. The 24-hour transit clause sits alongside it and answers a different question — injury or distress caused by the journey itself, which is neither congenital nor infectious but is still ours to put right.</p>

          <h5>Congenital Defects and Infectious Disease Are Both Covered</h5>
          <p>We separate the two deliberately because they fail differently. A congenital fault is usually found at the first examination and is unambiguous. An infection may take a few days to declare itself, which is why the window runs to 72 hours rather than 24 — long enough for an incubating organism to become detectable, short enough that a new household has not yet become a competing explanation.</p>

          <h6>In Writing: The Exact Conditions Named on Your Guarantee</h6>
          <p>The document you receive names congenital defects and infectious disease explicitly, states the 72-hour health window and the 24-hour shipping window as separate clauses, and records your grey's closed band number so the terms attach to one specific bird. <a href="/african-grey-parrot-health-guarantee/">Our full health-guarantee policy</a> carries the complete wording, including the fine print this summary compresses.</p>

          <h3>What the Guarantee Does Not Cover, Stated Plainly</h3>
          <p>Four exclusions, and we would rather you read them now than discover them during a claim. The band must stay on. The diet must be the one we send you home with, not a seed bowl. No other birds for the first thirty days. And the avian-vet examination has to happen inside the window — without it, the sale is as-is.</p>

          <h4>Why Do Injury, Diet and Exposure Void the Guarantee?</h4>
          <p>Each exclusion marks a boundary where evidence runs out. Once a band is removed, no paperwork can be tied to that parrot. Once another bird shares the airspace, an infection has a second possible origin. Once a grey has been fed badly for a fortnight, its bloodwork tells a story about your kitchen rather than our aviary. These are not escape hatches; they are the honest edge of what anyone can promise about an animal in someone else's home.</p>
        </section>


        <div class="seam-wrap"><img class="seam" src={seam} alt="" loading="lazy" width="182" height="60" decoding="async"></div>

        <!-- ===================== §6 · THE REFRAME + TABLE E ===================== -->
        <section class="sec" id="window">
          <h2>Is a 72-Hour Guarantee Long Enough, or Do You Need a Full Year?</h2>
          <p class="opener">Guarantee length has turned into an advertising arms race among African Grey sellers, and here at C.A.Gs we have watched it run in the wrong direction. The listings offering a full year are, with striking consistency, the ones least able to honour anything — so the number on the badge tells you less than how the promise is anchored.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "72-hour-vs-one-year-parrot-health-guarantee.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "72-hour-vs-one-year-parrot-health-guarantee-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "72-hour-vs-one-year-parrot-health-guarantee.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="Comparing a vet-anchored 72-hour parrot guarantee against unenforceable one-year and 365-day marketing promises">

          <h3>Why a Longer Guarantee Is Often the Weaker One</h3>
          <p>Length costs a seller nothing to advertise and everything to honour, so the incentive runs toward inflation. Independent parrot guides reach the same conclusion from the other side, advising buyers to treat an unusually long guarantee as a prompt to be reasonable rather than reassured. A promise stretched across twelve months has to survive a year of someone else's husbandry — which is another way of saying it will almost never be paid out.</p>

          <h4>What Does a "1-Year" or "365-Day" Guarantee Really Promise?</h4>
          <p>Read the ones we found and the pattern repeats: a long window, no list of covered conditions, no named remedy, and no requirement that anyone examine the bird. The length is doing the persuading while the substance stays blank. One page-one seller pairs a one-year badge with a Congo discounted to roughly $750 — a figure that cannot cover the testing the badge implies.</p>

          <h5>An Unenforceable Promise Protects the Seller, Not the Buyer</h5>
          <p>A guarantee only functions if a third party can establish what was wrong and when. Strip out the vet requirement and there is nothing to establish anything with, which leaves the seller as sole judge of their own liability. That is why we would rather hand you a short window with an independent examiner in it than a long one where we decide.</p>

          <h6>From the Vet: Why a Fast, Documented Window Protects Your Grey</h6>
          <p>Avian veterinarians make the clinical case better than the marketing does: a parrot that has just flown needs eyes on it quickly regardless of paperwork, because birds mask illness until it is advanced. <a href="https://www.aav.org/search/custom.asp?id=1803" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Association of Avian Veterinarians' find-a-vet directory</a> is where to book that appointment ↗, ideally before your grey travels rather than after.</p>

          <h3>How Our 72-Hour Window Compares to the Sellers Beside Us</h3>
          <p>Set the models side by side on the axes that decide a claim, not the one printed on the badge.</p>
