        <!-- ===================== §7 · THE CLAIM PATH ===================== -->
        <section class="sec" id="claim">
          <h2>What Happens If Your African Grey Arrives or Falls Ill?</h2>
          <p class="opener">A guarantee is only as good as the path through it, so here at C.A.Gs we keep that path to three steps and no intermediaries. You see a vet, you send us what the vet found, and we settle it — with Mark or Teri answering, not a form.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "african-grey-health-guarantee-claim-steps.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "african-grey-health-guarantee-claim-steps-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "african-grey-health-guarantee-claim-steps.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="The three steps for using an African Grey health guarantee, from avian-vet exam to refund or replacement">

          <h3>The Three Steps From First Symptom to Resolution</h3>
          <p>Book the avian-vet appointment first — it is the step everything else depends on. Ask for the diagnosis in writing, including any test results, and forward it to us the same day. We then settle the claim as replacement or refund. Nothing about that sequence requires you to argue with us; the veterinary record does the arguing.</p>

          <h4>How Soon Must You Book the Avian-Vet Exam?</h4>
          <p>Inside 72 hours of your grey arriving, which is why we ask buyers to line up a practice before travel day. Avian vets are thinner on the ground than general practices and often booked a week out. If the only appointment you can get falls outside the window, tell us before it lapses rather than after — we can work with a booked appointment, but not with a lapsed window and no examination.</p>

          <h5>The Shipping Window Is 24 Hours, the Health Window 72</h5>
          <p>Two clocks, both starting when you take possession. The shorter one covers anything the journey caused and closes quickly because transit effects show immediately. The longer one covers the congenital and infectious categories and runs three times as long because an incubating organism needs time to become detectable.</p>

          <h6>In Writing: Replacement or Refund, and Who Decides</h6>
          <p>The document states replacement or refund at our discretion. In practice the choice usually makes itself — a family who has waited months for a specific chick generally wants another bird, while someone whose circumstances have changed wants their money. We ask which you would prefer before we decide.</p>

          <h3>Why We Ask You to See an Avian Vet Right Away</h3>
          <p>Partly because it is what makes the guarantee enforceable, and partly because it is simply good practice. That first examination gives your grey a baseline weight, a baseline bloodwork picture and a relationship with a practice that may look after him for forty years. Buyers who skip it are usually the ones who later cannot tell whether something is new.</p>
        </section>

