        <!-- ===================== §9 · THE DOCUMENTATION STACK ===================== -->
        <section class="sec" id="documents">
          <h2>What Documentation Backs Every Guaranteed Grey?</h2>
          <p class="opener">A guarantee with nothing behind it is a sentence on a website. Here at C.A.Gs it sits on top of a five-document stack, and each of those documents is independently checkable by somebody who is not us — which is the only property that makes any of them worth having.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "documentation-with-guaranteed-african-grey.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "documentation-with-guaranteed-african-grey-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "documentation-with-guaranteed-african-grey.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="The five documents behind a guaranteed grey parrot — guarantee, vet certificate, DNA certificate, CITES papers and closed band">

          <h3>The Paper Stack Standing Behind the Guarantee</h3>
          <p>The written guarantee names the terms. The avian-vet health certificate records the pre-sale examination and is dated within ten days of travel. The DNA sexing certificate names the hen or cock. The CITES Appendix I documentation establishes captive-bred origin. The closed leg band ties all four to one parrot. Remove any one of them and the remaining four lose some of their force.</p>

          <h4>How Does Each Document Protect Your Purchase?</h4>
          <p>Differently, and that is the point of having five rather than one. The vet certificate protects you at the moment of sale; the guarantee protects you for three days afterwards; the DNA certificate settles a question that visual inspection cannot; the CITES paperwork protects you at a state line or a vet's reception desk years from now; the band makes every other document provably about your bird.</p>

          <h5>Vet Certificate, DNA Certificate, CITES Papers and Band Together</h5>
          <p>Read as a set, they answer the four questions a buyer actually has — is this bird healthy, what sex is it, is owning it lawful, and is this paperwork really about this parrot. No single document answers more than one. Sellers who publish one badge and call it documentation are answering a quarter of the question.</p>

          <h6>On File: What Each Document Proves and Who Issues It</h6>
          <p>The guarantee comes from us; the health certificate from a board-certified avian veterinarian; the sexing certificate from the testing laboratory; the CITES documentation from the captive-breeding record; the band from the closed ring fitted at a few weeks old. Four of the five originate outside this aviary, which is precisely why they carry weight.</p>

