        <!-- ===================== §10 · DISEASE SCREENING ===================== -->
        <section class="sec" id="screening">
          <h2>How Does One Avian-Vet Exam Screen for the Big Three Diseases?</h2>
          <p class="opener">The pre-sale examination is where our guarantee stops being a promise and starts being a measurement. One visit, one sample, and three PCR panels come back — which is how we can afford to stand behind infectious disease at all.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "one-vet-exam-three-disease-screens-parrot.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "one-vet-exam-three-disease-screens-parrot-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "one-vet-exam-three-disease-screens-parrot.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="One avian-vet examination PCR-screens a grey parrot for beak and feather disease, polyomavirus and psittacosis">

          <h3>Which Diseases We PCR-Screen Before a Grey Ships</h3>
          <p>Psittacine beak and feather disease, avian polyomavirus, and psittacosis. All three run by polymerase chain reaction off the same sample type the sexing assay uses, so one clean pull answers several questions at once. A grey that returns anything other than a clear result does not go on this page.</p>
          <img class="sec-img og-photo" src={IMG + "african-grey-avian-vet-exam-beak-nails-wings.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "african-grey-avian-vet-exam-beak-nails-wings-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "african-grey-avian-vet-exam-beak-nails-wings.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="One of our greys at her avian-vet appointment having her beak, nails and wings attended to">
          <p class="cap">One of ours at the practice — beak and nails tidied, wings trimmed, and the pre-sale panel drawn in the same visit.</p>

          <h4>Why Do PBFD, Polyoma and Psittacosis Matter Most?</h4>
          <p>Because each is transmissible, each can be silent in a young bird, and each changes what a household should do next. <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/exotic-and-laboratory-animals/pet-birds/viral-diseases-of-pet-birds" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Veterinary overviews of psittacine beak and feather disease</a> describe a virus that can sit undeclared for months ↗. Psittacosis matters for a further reason — it passes to people, and <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/psittacosis/about/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">public-health guidance on Chlamydia psittaci</a> treats it as a reportable illness ↗. Screening for it before a bird ships is as much about your family as your parrot.</p>
        </section>

