        <!-- ===================== §11 · SCAM / LEGITIMACY ===================== -->
        <section class="sec" id="scam">
          <h2>How Do You Know an African Grey Breeder Is Legitimate, Not a Scam?</h2>
          <p class="opener">Most people reading a guarantee page are really asking a blunter question, and here at C.A.Gs we would rather answer it directly than pretend it was not asked. The African Grey market carries an unusual volume of fraud, and a guarantee badge is one of the cheapest things for a fraudulent listing to display.</p>
          <img class="sec-img og-photo" src={IMG + "african-grey-hand-held-tame-not-a-scam-bird.webp"}
               srcset={IMG + "african-grey-hand-held-tame-not-a-scam-bird-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "african-grey-hand-held-tame-not-a-scam-bird.webp 1408w"}
               sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
               alt="A tame hand-held grey parrot from our aviary, the kind of photograph a fraudulent listing cannot produce on request">

          <h3>The Red Flags Reddit Buyers Learn Too Late</h3>
          <p>Owner forums are full of the same post written by different people: a price well under market, a seller who will not video-call, pressure toward a wire transfer or a peer-to-peer payment app, photographs that reverse-search to somebody else's bird, and an offer to ship internationally. Any one of these is a reason to slow down. Two together is an answer. <a href="/how-to-avoid-african-grey-parrot-scams/">Our full scam-avoidance guide</a> works through each tell in detail.</p>

          <h4>Why Won't a Real Breeder Sell a Healthy Grey for $800?</h4>
          <p>Because the input costs are fixed and public. Months of hand-feeding, an avian-vet examination, three PCR panels, a sexing assay and CITES paperwork do not compress to fit an $800 sticker. When a listing pairs that price with a one-year guarantee, the two claims are contradicting each other in the same sentence.</p>

          <h5>A Sub-Floor Price Is the Cheapest Scam Signal to Spot</h5>
          <p>It requires no expertise to check, which is what makes it useful. You do not need to evaluate a photograph or interpret a certificate — you only need to know roughly what the species costs. That is why we publish our floor rather than quoting privately: a published floor is a number you can measure other sellers against.</p>

          <h6>From the Vet: What an $800 "Healthy" Grey Usually Hides</h6>
          <p>Where such a bird exists at all, the saving has come from somewhere — an unweaned chick sold early, a skipped disease panel, a wild-sourced or undocumented bird, or a parrot rehomed for a behavioural reason nobody disclosed. Each of those becomes a veterinary bill or a heartbreak later, which is the arithmetic the sticker price hides.</p>

          <h3>How a Documented Breeder Answers Every Question Without Flinching</h3>
          <p>Ask us for a live video call with a specific bird and we will schedule one. Ask which laboratory ran the panels and we will name it. Ask for the licence, the address, or a photograph of a band and none of it is a problem. The test is not whether a seller has good answers ready — it is whether questions are welcome at all. <a href="/trusted-african-grey-parrot-breeders/">Our breeder credentials page</a> sets out who we are in more detail.</p>
        </section>

