          <h3>What the Price Buys Beyond the Bird</h3>
          <p>Months of hand-feeding and socialising. A board-certified avian-vet examination. Three PCR disease panels. A DNA sexing assay and its certificate. CITES Appendix I documentation and a closed band. And the written guarantee itself, which is only affordable to offer because everything preceding it has already reduced the risk. <a href="/african-grey-parrot-price/">Our itemised African Grey pricing page</a> breaks each line out.</p>
          <p>Set against the life of the bird, the figure looks different again. An African Grey lives 40 to 60 years in good care, so the purchase price is a rounding error beside four or five decades of food, veterinary care and enrichment. The 72-hour window exists to protect the start of that span — but the reason we screen and document so heavily is the other fifty years, which no guarantee of any length can cover.</p>

          <h4>Which Costs Would a Sub-Floor Seller Have to Skip?</h4>
          <p>Work backwards from $800 and the answer is unavoidable. The disease panels go first because they are invisible to a buyer. Then the vet examination. Then weaning time, since every extra week is feed and labour. What survives is a young, untested bird with a badge on its listing — which is exactly the animal that generates the veterinary bill a guarantee was supposed to protect you from.</p>
