          <p class="opener">Yes — a captive-bred African Grey with proper documentation is lawful to own and transfer domestically, and here at C.A.Gs every bird we place is captive-bred in the USA with that paperwork in hand.</p>

          <h3>Why Your CITES Papers Outlast the Guarantee Window</h3>
          <p><a href="https://cites.org/eng/gallery/species/bird/grey_parrot.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The CITES listing for <em>Psittacus erithacus</em></a> placed the species on Appendix I in 2017 ↗, the strictest tier, in response to pressure on wild populations. That listing governs international trade; it does not prohibit domestic ownership of a captive-bred bird. What it does mean is that documentation stops being a nicety — your CITES paperwork and closed band are what establish your grey's captive-bred origin, and they are worth keeping for the bird's whole life. We have never sold a wild-caught bird and never will.</p>
